  "query_preview": "..Mid- to upper-level clouds are in place this morning\nacross much of Utah and southwest Wyoming with largely stable\nconditions and temperatures reaching just a few degrees below \nnormal. As a fast-moving trough sweeps across the Great Basin \ntonight, modest moisture will gradually begin to infiltrate the \nlower levels, resulting in low precipitation chances on Monday \nprimarily across higher terrain. Given weak dynamics and very \nlimited moisture, rainfall/liquid equivalent will largely remain \nbelow 0.10\" (90% chance).\n\nAn even drier air mass will move into the region following this\nstorm system, with a ridge crossing the state Tuesday into \nWednesday. Depending on how quickly low-level clouds are scoured \nout Monday night, fog-prone areas such as the Cache Valley could \nsee patchy fog development by Tuesday morning given stable \nconditions, light winds, and potentially clearing skies. \nOtherwise, expect quiet weather through at least mid-week.\n\nModel guidance continues to trend drier with a weak trough moving\nthrough late Wednesday into Thursday, but this thankfully means\nlittle to no impactful weather for holiday travel and enough\nmixing to ward off valley inversion development. Heading into \nthis weekend, however, chances for precipitation will begin to \nincrease. Forecast confidence is still low, as ultimately\nprecipitation chances will depend on the trajectory of moisture \nfrom a landfalling atmospheric river in Oregon late this week. \nCurrently, ensemble guidance appears to favor a more southwesterly\ntrajectory which would result in moisture landing a bit too far \nnorth, but the deterministic GFS has a more zonal orientation, \ndrawing moisture into northern Utah this weekend.",
